  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n- Non-zero chance of rain for southern areas into Tuesday\n\nThe frontal system that brought the soaking rain to much of the \nforecast area over this past weekend, has sank well south of the \narea. This has taken all of its rain with it, leaving our area dry. \nWe are still seeing quite a bit of cloud cover over the area with \nthe long wave upper trough/low still to our west. These clouds are \nmainly high clouds north of the I-94 corridor, with mid level clouds \nalong and south of I-94. \n\nThe upper low is expected to slowly rotate/dig to the SE through \nTuesday. For the most part, it will keep just about all of any \nweather with it to the south. The exception to this is the far \nsouthern row of counties where the mid level moisture is, and a \nlittle closer to any short waves with the parent wave. The dry low \nlevel feed of air from the NE will mitigate the chances a bit. Can't \nrule out a little stronger shower being able to produce some light \nrain fall that would survive the dry air to the ground during peak \nheating today and Tuesday. \n\n- High confidence of dry and seasonable weather mid-week and beyond\n\nThere has been good run to run consistency and agreement amongst the \nmodels and ensemble members that we will be entering quite a dry \nperiod from Wednesday through at least next weekend.\n\nThe upper low is shown by most data to rotate toward the Mid-\nAtlantic area, while additional energy comes onshore over the \nPacific NW. What this means for Michigan is that we will be under \nthe influence of strengthening upper ridging as the upper air \npattern amplifies between the two upper lows near the two U.S. \ncoasts. This amplified pattern slows the progression of the systems, \nso we are looking at an extended period of the upper ridge directly \ncontrolling our weather. \n\nWe will remain a tad cool on Wednesday as the cold pool aloft is \nslow to move out. We will then see slow and gradual moderation of \nthe air mass due to building heights and weak warm air advection as \nthe cold pool exits completely. Highs should gradually warm into the \n70s toward the end of the week and next weekend, with cool and \ncomfortable overnight temps in the 40s and 50s with a dry air mass \nin place.",
